monster_from:
ZzzCharacter.cpp:14021-14027, CreateMonster, case MONSTER_ICE_MONSTER:

    OpenMonsterModel(MONSTER_MODEL_ICE_MONSTER);
    c = CreateCharacter(Key, MODEL_ICE_MONSTER, PositionX, PositionY);
    c->Object.BlendMesh = 0;
    c->Object.BlendMeshLight = 1.f;

No Scale (so 1.0), no weapon, no Level. BlendMesh 0 is the whole of this model: RenderMesh draws a mesh through the blend branch when `m->Texture == blendMeshIndex` (ZzzBMD.cpp:1509), and Monster16.bmd has one mesh on texture 0. See glow_from.

death_from:
It does not leave a corpse. MU plays MONSTER01_DIE to its end, and when AnimationCharacter finds the clip stopped it calls EtcStopAnimationSetting (ZzzCharacter.cpp:3713-3733), whose monster branch calls CreateBlood once (ZzzCharacter.cpp:3521-3528). CreateBlood (ZzzEffectBlurSpark.cpp:445-455):

    if (o->Type == MODEL_ICE_MONSTER)
    {
        o->Live = false;
        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
            CreateEffect(MODEL_ICE_SMALL, o->Position, o->Angle, o->Light);
    }

The model stops drawing and ten of the wizard's Ice shards, subtype 0, are thrown from its feet -- the same shards src/game/fx/ice.h already ports, without the block. Unlike the Stone Golem's burst (CharacterDie), this waits for the death clip: 7 keys at 0.55 a reference frame, about half a second.
